          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            An
            %em orientifold
            (
            %a{:href => "#DaiLeighPolchinski89"}> Dai-Leigh-Polchinski 89, p. 12
            ) is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/target+space"} target
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-models"} sigma-models
            that combines aspects of
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ℤ
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}_2
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orbifold"} orbifold
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orientation"} orientation
              reversal
            on the worldsheet, hence the name.
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+II+string+theory"} type II string theory
            orientifold backgrounds (inducing e.g.
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+I+string+theory"} type I string theory
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sugimoto+string"} Sugimoto string
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ℤ
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}_2
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fixed+points"} fixed points
            – called
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/O-planes"} O-planes
            (see there for more) – are required for
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/RR-field+tadpole+cancellation"} RR-field tadpole cancellation
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intersecting+D-brane+models"} intersecting D-brane models
            used in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+phenomenology"} string phenomenology
          %p
            Where generally (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+gauge+field"}> higher gauge
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} fields
            in
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocycles"} cocycles
            in (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology+theory"}> differential
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology+theory"} cohomology theory
            and typically in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+oriented+cohomology+theory"} complex oriented cohomology theory
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ℤ
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}_2
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+cohomology"} equivariant cohomology
            and typically in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real-oriented+cohomology+theory"} real-oriented cohomology theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B-field"} B-field
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+differential+cohomology"} ordinary
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology"} differential cohomology
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/HZR-theory"} HZR-theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/RR-fields"} RR-fields
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+cohomology"} twisted
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+K-theory"} differential
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-theory"} K-theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KR-theory"} KR-theory
            (
            %a{:href => "#Witten98"}> Witten 98
            ).
          %p
            An explicit model for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B-fields"} B-fields
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bosonic+string"} bosonic string
            on orientifolds (differential
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/HZR-theory"} HZR-theory
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Jandl"} Schreiber-Schweigert-Waldorf 05
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#GawedzkiSuszekWaldorf08"} Gawedzki-Suszek-Waldorf 08
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#HMSV16"} HMSV 16
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#HMSV19"} HMSV 19
          %p
            The claim that for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superstring"} superstring
            the B-field is more generally a cocycle with coefficients in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Picard+infinity-group"} Picard infinity-group
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+K-theory"} complex K-theory
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+line+2-bundles"}> super line 2-bundles
            ) and a detailed discussion of the orientifold version of this can be found in (
            %a{:href => "#Precis"}> Distler-Freed-Moore 09
            ,
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#DistlerFreedMooreII"} Distler-Freed-Moore 10
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Freed"} Freed 12
          %p
            The quadratic pairing entering the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/11d+Chern-Simons+theory"} 11d Chern-Simons theory
            that governs the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/RR-field"} RR-field
            here as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/self-dual+higher+gauge+field"} self-dual higher gauge field
            is given in (
            %a{:href => "#Precis"}> DFM 10, def. 6
            ).
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#OrientifoldBackreactionOrNot Orientifold backreaction?
          %p
            Essentially all existing results on orientifolds (such as
            = succeed "-charges" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/O-plane"} O-plane
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/RR-field+tadpole+cancellation"} RR-field tadpole cancellation
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-models"} sigma-models
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+orbifold"} flat orbifold
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/toroidal+orbifold"}> toroidal orbifold
            ) or orientifolded
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Calabi-Yau+manifold"} Calabi-Yau manifold
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/target+spacetimes"} target spacetimes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+CFT"} rational CFT
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+vacua"} string vacua
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gepner+models"}> Gepner models
            ,
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-geometric+vacua"} non-geometric vacua
          %h4#NoResultOnBackreactedOrientifolds No string-theory results on back-reacted orientifolds
          %p
            There are to date no results in actual
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            for what one might expect to be the curved back-reacted geometry of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orientifolds"} orientifolds
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/near+horizon+geometry"} near horizon geometry
            that is well-known for the case of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/black+brane"} black
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-branes"} D-branes
          %p
            From
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "#BanksBroeck06"} Banks-van den Broeck 06
          %blockquote
            %p
              The discussion of these
              = succeed "orientifold" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
              compactifications is generally carried out in low energy
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+field+theory"} effective field theory
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0412277"} 3a
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0506066"} 3b
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0412129"} 4
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orientifold"} orientifold
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singularities"} singularities
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+string+theory"} perturbative world sheet treatment
              of these backgrounds.
            %p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [...]
            %p
              The inevitable orientifold of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+compactifications"} flux compactifications
              is one potential barrier to an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+field+theory"} effective field theory
              treatment
              = succeed "footnote" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Greg+Moore"} G. Moore
              and S. Ramanujam have emphasized to us the problems with the back reaction of the orientifold
              = succeed "]" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mo .
                      %mo .
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [...
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
          %p
            From
            %a{:href => "#CordovaLucaTomasiello19"} Cordova-de Luca-Tomasiello 19
            (p. 2 and p. 30):
          %blockquote
            %p
              To assess the validity of these
              = succeed "assumed" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
              solutions in
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality+in+string+theory"} dual
              description. Unfortunately neither of these options is available
            %p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [...]
            %p
              the presence of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/O-planes"} O-planes
              is inferred by comparison with their flat-space behavior. Since the
              = succeed "spacetimes" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
              have strong curvature and coupling,
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+curvature+corrections"} stringy corrections
              come into play, and it is impossible to decide with
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
              alone whether the solutions are valid.
            %p
              It is important to stress that this will be so for any solution with
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/O-planes"} O-planes
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/O-plane"} O-plane
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singularities"} singularities
            %p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [...]
            %p We clearly need alternative procedures that are better justified physically.
          %h4#AnAssumptionInEffectiveFieldTheory A popular assumption in effective field theory
          %p
            Nevertheless, motivated from the fact that the computations for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+orbifolds"} flat orbifolds
            show that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/O-planes"} O-planes
            there are similar to (while clearly different from!)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-branes"} D-branes
            (
            %em not
            in their back-reacted form as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/black+branes"} black branes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/negative+number"} negative
            tension (i.e. negative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/energy"} energy
            density) it may seem plausible that the low energy
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+field+theory"} effective field theory
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+string+theory+vacua"} perturbative string theory vacua
            including
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/O-planes"} O-planes
            is a modification of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            where negative-energy source-terms are added to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equations+of+motion"} equations of motion
            much like one might add
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/black+brane"} black
            = succeed "-contributions," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-brane"} D-brane
          %p
            This
            %em ad hoc
            effective field theory picture of orientifold backgrounds has been advocated, seminally, in
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#GiddingsKachruPolchinski01"} Giddings-Kachru-Polchinski 01
          %blockquote
            %p
              String theory does have such
              = succeed "negative" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
              objects, and so evades the no-go theorem
              = succeed "which" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
          %p
            There this is followed by reference to a presumed example considered earlier in
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Verlinde00"} Verlinde 00
          %blockquote
            %p
              In a more complete treatment, we must also include the backreaction of the 64 orientifold planes. These have a negative tension equal to −1/4 times the D3-brane tension, which need to be taken into account. We can write an explicit form for the background metric
              = succeed "just" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
          %p
            One may feel this is plausible – and it might even be right, sometimes – but there does not exist a derivation of this statement from actual
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+string+theory"} perturbative string theory
            (see
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#NoResultOnBackreactedOrientifolds"} above
          %h4#UseInLandscapeSwamplandLiterature Its use in the landscape/swampland literature
          %p
            Nevertheless, in the wake of the discussion of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/landscape+of+string+theory+vacua"} landscape
            (or not) of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Sitter+spacetime"} de Sitter
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory+vacua"} string theory vacua
            and of the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/swampland+conjectures"}> swampland conjectures
            ” it became popular to rely on the handwaving argument of
            %a{:href => "#GiddingsKachruPolchinski01"} Giddings-Kachru-Polchinski 01
            and behave as if it is established that questions about
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+field+theory"} low energy effective
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orientifold"} orientifold
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory+vacua"} string theory vacua
            may be answered using a modification of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            where the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equations+of+motion"} equations of motion
            are changed – by hand – simply by including negative-tension source terms of some form.
          %p
            This step happens for instance around (2.2) in
            %a{:href => "#Junghans20"} Junghans 20
            where it is justified, without references, by the words “as is standard in the literature” (footnote 5 in
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Junghans20"} Junghans 20
          %p
            %br/
          %h3#lifts_to_mtheory_ftheory Lifts to M-theory F-theory
          %p
            Lifts of orientifold background from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+II+string+theory"} type II string theory
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/F-theory"} F-theory
            go back to (
            %a{:href => "#Sen96"}> Sen 96
            ,
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Sen97a"} Sen 97a
          %p
            Lifts of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+IIA+string+theory"} type IIA string theory
            orientifolds of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D6-branes"} D6-branes
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ADE+singularities"} D-type ADE singularities
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory"} M-theory
            (through the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality+between+M-theory+and+type+IIA+string+theory"} duality between M-theory and type IIA string theory
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Sen97b"} Sen 97b
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/heterotic+M-theory+on+ADE-orbifolds"} heterotic M-theory on ADE-orbifolds
          %p
            A more general scan of possible lifts of type IIA orientifolds to M-theory is indicated in (
            %a{:href => "#HananyKol00"}> Hanany-Kol 00, around (3.2)
            ), see (
            %a{:href => "#HuertaSatiSchreiber18"}> Huerta-Sati-Schreiber 18, Prop. 4.7
            ) for details.
          %p
            For instance the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/O4-plane"} O4-plane
            lifts to the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/MO5-plane"} MO5-plane
          %p (…)
